7
00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:19.692
Ch 09, Ch 17, Ch 18, Ch 19, What you will build:

8
00:00:19.972 --> 00:00:26.543
How the grocery platform is watched, logs, metrics and traces, and the SLOs and alerts on top.

9
00:00:26.823 --> 00:00:33.927
One lab uses a real Prometheus scraping a real service; the rest build the mechanics from scratch and measure them.

10
00:00:34.207 --> 00:00:37.129
L1: structured logs and correlation IDs.

11
00:00:37.409 --> 00:00:50.977
Over 24,776 log events, a correlation id reconstructs one failed request's path across 5 services in one query; without it the 5 lines are lost in the pile.

12
00:00:51.257 --> 00:01:03.375
Trace-based sampling cut INFO volume to 3% while keeping every error, and a (service, level, userid) label would have made 18,530 time series versus 10.

13
00:01:04.725 --> 00:01:07.242
L2: metrics, with real Prometheus.

14
00:01:07.522 --> 00:01:14.263
A service exposes a Prometheus histogram; Prometheus scrapes it and computes histogramquantile live.

15
00:01:14.543 --> 00:01:22.757
The catch: p99 from the histogram was +26% off the exact value because the tail lands in a wide bucket.

16
00:01:23.037 --> 00:01:31.805
The average (74 ms) met a 200 ms SLO while p99 was 1,545 ms.

17
00:01:32.085 --> 00:01:33.983
L3: distributed tracing.

18
00:01:34.263 --> 00:01:42.605
A span tree finds the critical path, the slow fx-rates call under pricing, and shows that optimizing anything off it changes nothing.

19
00:01:42.885 --> 00:01:47.087
Aggregating 10,000 traces names the p99 driver.

20
00:01:47.367 --> 00:01:55.538
Head sampling at 1% kept 6 of 554 slow traces; tail sampling kept all 554.

21
00:01:56.888 --> 00:01:57.763
L4:

22
00:01:58.043 --> 00:01:59.963
SLOs and burn-rate alerting.

23
00:02:00.243 --> 00:02:19.229
On 30 days with 3 injected incidents, a naive threshold raised a false page, a single long window flapped into 17 false pages, and Google SRE's multi-window multi-burn-rate paged 4 times, 0 false, ~2-min detect, each page tied to real budget burn.

24
00:02:19.509 --> 00:02:20.491
Reading time:

25
00:02:20.771 --> 00:02:22.691
120 min + code.

26
00:02:24.041 --> 00:02:24.894
Part 1.

27
00:02:25.174 --> 00:02:27.094
Introduction and Why It Matters.

28
00:02:28.344 --> 00:02:30.563
A story: the dashboard was all green.

29
00:02:30.843 --> 00:02:35.493
Checkout conversion dropped 8% one evening, but every dashboard was green.

30
00:02:35.773 --> 00:02:45.352
The post-incident review found the platform was monitoring (pre-set dashboards of averages) but not observable (able to ask new questions of the data):

31
00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:47.915
The latency dashboard showed the average:

32
00:02:48.195 --> 00:02:49.496
74 ms.

33
00:02:49.776 --> 00:02:51.973
Well under the 200 ms target.

34
00:02:52.253 --> 00:03:02.429
But the average hid the tail, p99 was over 1.5 seconds (L2), because a currency-conversion dependency under pricing was intermittently slow.

35
00:03:02.709 --> 00:03:04.267
Averages can't see tails.

36
00:03:05.617 --> 00:03:09.115
There was no way to follow one failed checkout across services.

37
00:03:09.395 --> 00:03:19.379
Logs were free-text sentences with no shared id, so reconstructing a single request meant grepping five services and guessing by timestamp (L1).

38
00:03:19.659 --> 00:03:22.539
The alert was error rate 1% for 5 minutes.

39
00:03:22.819 --> 00:03:28.985
It had paged 40 times that month, mostly on harmless off-peak blips, so the on-call had muted it.

40
00:03:29.265 --> 00:03:33.958
The one real slow-burn incident it should have caught, it detected late (L4).

41
00:03:35.308 --> 00:03:37.313
Nobody could point to the slow service.

42
00:03:37.593 --> 00:03:50.628
Metrics said pricing is slow sometimes; only a trace showed the slowness was a nested fx-rates call, on the critical path just 30% of the time but dominating p99 when it was (L3).

43
00:03:50.908 --> 00:04:08.103
The rebuild: structured logs with a trace id on every line, RED metrics with proper histograms, distributed tracing with exemplars linking a slow metric to its trace, and SLO-based multi-window burn-rate alerts that page on budget threats, not noise.

44
00:04:09.453 --> 00:04:10.135
Quote:

45
00:04:10.415 --> 00:04:12.079
The thesis of this chapter.

46
00:04:12.359 --> 00:04:16.626
Monitoring answers questions you knew to ask (a dashboard of averages).

47
00:04:16.906 --> 00:04:27.125
Observability lets you ask new ones during an incident you didn't foresee, which needs high-cardinality, correlated logs, metrics and traces that share a request id.

48
00:04:27.405 --> 00:04:31.309
Measure percentiles, never averages, for anything user-facing.

49
00:04:31.589 --> 00:04:41.189
Define SLOs as the contract, spend an error budget, and alert on how fast you're burning it, so pages mean a human must act now, not a number wiggled.

50
00:04:42.539 --> 00:04:44.843
Where this lives in the grocery platform.

