96
00:06:44.634 --> 00:06:45.509
Part 2.

97
00:06:45.789 --> 00:06:47.666
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

98
00:06:48.916 --> 00:06:49.833
Intuition.

99
00:06:50.113 --> 00:06:56.513
Metrics are the car's dashboard; logs are the trip diary; traces are the GPS route of one journey.

100
00:06:56.793 --> 00:07:01.380
The dashboard (metrics) tells you that something is wrong cheaply and continuously.

101
00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:04.476
The diary (logs) tells you what happened in detail.

102
00:07:04.756 --> 00:07:07.892
The route (a trace) shows where in the journey the delay was.

103
00:07:08.172 --> 00:07:11.457
You need all three, sharing one trip number (the trace id).

104
00:07:11.737 --> 00:07:13.124
The average is the lie.

105
00:07:13.404 --> 00:07:20.871
If nine customers wait 20 ms and one waits 2 seconds, the average 218 ms describes nobody.

106
00:07:21.151 --> 00:07:24.180
The tenth customer is the one who abandons the cart.

107
00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:25.783
Watch percentiles.

108
00:07:27.133 --> 00:07:29.394
An SLO is a promise with a budget.

109
00:07:29.674 --> 00:07:36.394
99.9% of checkouts succeed allows 0.1% to fail, that's the error budget.

110
00:07:36.674 --> 00:07:41.218
As long as you're within it, you can ship; when you're burning it fast, you stop and fix.

111
00:07:41.498 --> 00:07:47.706
Alerts watch the burn rate, like a fuel gauge that only warns when you'll run dry before the destination.

112
00:07:49.056 --> 00:07:50.229
The three signals.

113
00:07:50.509 --> 00:07:51.853
Here is a quick table:

114
00:07:52.133 --> 00:07:55.547
Signal, and What, and Cost, and Cardinality, and Best for.

115
00:07:55.827 --> 00:07:56.701
Metrics.

116
00:07:56.981 --> 00:07:57.536
What:

117
00:07:57.816 --> 00:08:01.336
Numeric time series (counters, gauges, histograms).

118
00:08:01.616 --> 00:08:02.341
Cost:

119
00:08:02.621 --> 00:08:04.243
Cheap, constant per series.

120
00:08:04.523 --> 00:08:05.504
Cardinality:

121
00:08:05.784 --> 00:08:08.003
Must be low (bounded label values).

122
00:08:08.283 --> 00:08:09.136
Best for:

123
00:08:09.416 --> 00:08:10.419
Is it broken?

124
00:08:10.699 --> 00:08:11.616
How much?

125
00:08:11.896 --> 00:08:13.411
Trends, SLOs, alerts.

126
00:08:13.691 --> 00:08:14.523
Logs.

127
00:08:14.803 --> 00:08:15.357
What:

128
00:08:15.637 --> 00:08:17.237
Timestamped structured events.

129
00:08:17.517 --> 00:08:18.243
Cost:

130
00:08:18.523 --> 00:08:20.144
Medium, high (volume).

131
00:08:20.424 --> 00:08:21.405
Cardinality:

132
00:08:21.685 --> 00:08:22.859
High (any field).

133
00:08:23.139 --> 00:08:23.992
Best for:

134
00:08:24.272 --> 00:08:26.043
What exactly happened to this thing?

135
00:08:26.323 --> 00:08:27.219
Traces.

136
00:08:27.499 --> 00:08:28.053
What:

137
00:08:28.333 --> 00:08:31.405
Causally-linked spans of one request across services.

138
00:08:31.685 --> 00:08:32.411
Cost:

139
00:08:32.691 --> 00:08:34.056
Medium (sampled).

140
00:08:34.336 --> 00:08:35.317
Cardinality:

141
00:08:35.597 --> 00:08:36.280
High.

142
00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:37.413
Best for:

143
00:08:37.693 --> 00:08:40.275
Where did the time or error go across services?

144
00:08:41.625 --> 00:08:53.678
They converge: an exemplar attaches a trace id to a metric bucket (jump from a slow p99 to a slow trace); logs carry the trace id (jump from a trace to its logs).

145
00:08:53.958 --> 00:08:57.222
This is the single pane that makes a system observable.

146
00:08:57.502 --> 00:09:11.241
OpenTelemetry (OTel) is the vendor-neutral standard: one set of SDKs and a Collector emit all three signals, with W3C Trace Context (traceparent) propagating the trace across services.

147
00:09:11.521 --> 00:09:17.089
In Java, Micrometer is the metrics façade (and bridges to OTel or Prometheus).

148
00:09:17.369 --> 00:09:19.417
Instrument once; export anywhere.

149
00:09:20.767 --> 00:09:22.751
Structured logging and correlation.

150
00:09:23.031 --> 00:09:33.377
Structured (JSON or logfmt), not prose. level:ERROR,service:inventory,traceid:...sku:...error:timeout is queryable by field; a sentence is not.

151
00:09:33.657 --> 00:09:41.231
L1: which requests failed in inventory, and why is one field filter on structured logs, and brittle regex on text.

152
00:09:41.511 --> 00:09:44.177
Correlation or trace id on every line.

153
00:09:44.457 --> 00:09:52.052
Generated at the edge, propagated through every service and put in every log line (via MDC or a logging context).

154
00:09:52.332 --> 00:10:05.175
L1: with a trace id, one query returns a failed request's ordered path across 5 services; without it, those 5 lines sit anonymously among 24,776.

155
00:10:06.525 --> 00:10:07.826
Levels and sampling.

156
00:10:08.106 --> 00:10:11.583
Keep ERROR or WARN always; sample INFO or DEBUG.

157
00:10:11.863 --> 00:10:16.173
Sample by trace, not by line, so a kept request keeps all its lines.

158
00:10:16.453 --> 00:10:24.773
L1: keeping 1% of INFO traces cut volume to 3% while keeping 100% of errors (and error traces whole).

159
00:10:25.053 --> 00:10:28.530
Never log secrets or full PII (Ch 23).

160
00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:31.583
Redact; log a token's id, not the token.

161
00:10:31.863 --> 00:10:33.698
Logs are expensive at volume.

162
00:10:33.978 --> 00:10:38.458
They're the highest-cost signal, so structure them, sample them, and set retention.

163
00:10:39.808 --> 00:10:42.560
Metrics: counters, gauges, histograms.

164
00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:45.912
Counter: monotonically increasing (requests, errors).

165
00:10:46.192 --> 00:10:49.691
You query its rate (rate(httprequeststotal5m)).

166
00:10:49.971 --> 00:10:53.832
Gauge: a value up or down (in-flight requests, queue depth, memory).

167
00:10:54.112 --> 00:11:00.896
Histogram: counts observations into buckets; lets you compute quantiles and how many under 200 ms.

168
00:11:01.176 --> 00:11:05.720
Prometheus histograms are cumulative (bucketle=... sum, count).

169
00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:13.104
Summary: client-computed quantiles; can't be aggregated across instances, so histograms are usually preferred.

170
00:11:14.454 --> 00:11:24.417
Percentiles from histograms are approximate. histogramquantile interpolates within the bucket holding the quantile's rank, so accuracy depends on bucket boundaries.

171
00:11:24.697 --> 00:11:27.598
L2 (real Prometheus over a real service):

172
00:11:28.948 --> 00:11:30.292
Here is a quick table:

173
00:11:30.572 --> 00:11:34.391
Quantile, and From histogram, and Exact, and Error. p50.

174
00:11:34.671 --> 00:11:35.759
From histogram:

175
00:11:36.039 --> 00:11:37.639
21.2 ms.

176
00:11:37.919 --> 00:11:38.772
Exact:

177
00:11:39.052 --> 00:11:40.673
20.7 ms.

178
00:11:40.953 --> 00:11:43.535
Error: +2%. p95.

179
00:11:43.815 --> 00:11:44.903
From histogram:

180
00:11:45.183 --> 00:11:46.953
86.7 ms.

181
00:11:47.233 --> 00:11:48.087
Exact:

182
00:11:48.367 --> 00:11:50.159
79.7 ms.

183
00:11:50.439 --> 00:11:53.063
Error: +9%. p99.

184
00:11:53.343 --> 00:11:54.431
From histogram:

185
00:11:54.711 --> 00:11:56.887
1,930 ms.

186
00:11:57.167 --> 00:11:58.020
Exact:

187
00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:00.860
1,545 ms.

188
00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:04.639
Error: +25%. p99.9.

189
00:12:04.919 --> 00:12:06.007
From histogram:

190
00:12:06.287 --> 00:12:08.655
2,443 ms.

191
00:12:08.935 --> 00:12:09.788
Exact:

192
00:12:10.068 --> 00:12:12.585
1,794 ms.

193
00:12:12.865 --> 00:12:14.785
Error: +36%.

194
00:12:16.135 --> 00:12:25.309
The p50 or p95 are close; the tail is coarse because it falls in the wide 1000 to 2500 ms bucket.

195
00:12:25.589 --> 00:12:34.847
Put buckets where your SLO lives (for example, around 200 ms), and remember you cannot recover accuracy the buckets never captured.

196
00:12:35.127 --> 00:12:43.490
(Prometheus native histograms, GA-ish in 2024 to 25, largely fix this with exponential auto-buckets.).

197
00:12:44.840 --> 00:12:45.885
RED and USE.

198
00:12:46.165 --> 00:12:49.621
Two complementary checklists so you instrument the right things:

199
00:12:49.901 --> 00:12:52.461
RED (services or request-driven):

200
00:12:52.741 --> 00:12:59.013
Rate (requests per second), Errors (failed per second or %), Duration (latency distribution).

201
00:12:59.293 --> 00:13:02.621
L2 computes all three from counters and a histogram.

202
00:13:02.901 --> 00:13:04.181
USE (resources):

203
00:13:04.461 --> 00:13:13.293
Utilization, Saturation (queue depth or wait), Errors, for CPU, memory, disks, connection pools, thread pools (Ch 19).

204
00:13:13.573 --> 00:13:18.651
RED tells you the service is unhealthy; USE tells you which resource is the cause.

205
00:13:18.931 --> 00:13:26.376
The four golden signals (Google SRE) are latency, traffic, errors, saturation, RED plus saturation.

206
00:13:27.726 --> 00:13:37.859
Averages versus percentiles: always chart p50 or p90 or p99 (and p99.9 for critical paths), never the mean.

207
00:13:38.139 --> 00:13:45.094
L2: a 74 ms average masked a 1,545 ms p99.

208
00:13:45.374 --> 00:13:51.305
Percentiles also don't average across instances, you need histograms to aggregate them correctly.

209
00:13:52.655 --> 00:13:54.041
Distributed tracing.

210
00:13:54.321 --> 00:14:03.089
A trace is a tree of spans; each span has a trace id, span id, parent id, service, operation, start or end and attributes.

211
00:14:03.369 --> 00:14:12.607
Context (traceparent) propagates across HTTP or gRPC or messaging so spans from different services join one trace.

212
00:14:12.887 --> 00:14:17.345
Critical path: the chain of spans that determines the end-to-end duration.

213
00:14:17.625 --> 00:14:27.375
L3 builds the product-page fan-out (Ch 19) and finds the slow request's critical path is bff, then pricing, then fx-rates.

214
00:14:27.655 --> 00:14:33.436
Optimizing anything off the critical path (catalog, inventory) does not shrink the request.

215
00:14:33.716 --> 00:14:36.767
This is the single most useful thing a trace tells you.

216
00:14:38.117 --> 00:14:52.986
Aggregate analysis: across 10,000 traces, L3 shows pricing or fx-rates sits on the critical path ~68% of the time and carries the whole tail (the 30% of calls that hit the slow currency service).

217
00:14:53.266 --> 00:14:57.127
That's the p99 lever, invisible to per-service metrics.

218
00:14:57.407 --> 00:15:07.605
Instrumentation: auto-instrumentation (OTel Java agent) covers HTTP or DB or messaging; add manual spans for business steps.

219
00:15:07.885 --> 00:15:12.514
Propagate context through async boundaries and thread pools (Ch 19:

220
00:15:12.794 --> 00:15:15.482
ScopedValue or context-wrapping executors).

221
00:15:16.832 --> 00:15:17.685
Sampling.

222
00:15:17.965 --> 00:15:21.528
Tracing every request is expensive at volume, so sample:

223
00:15:22.878 --> 00:15:24.222
Here is a quick table:

224
00:15:24.502 --> 00:15:28.619
Strategy, and When decided, and Keeps interesting traces?, and Cost.

225
00:15:28.899 --> 00:15:30.691
Head-based (probabilistic).

226
00:15:30.971 --> 00:15:32.038
When decided:

227
00:15:32.318 --> 00:15:34.217
At the start, on the trace id.

228
00:15:34.497 --> 00:15:36.033
Keeps interesting traces?:

229
00:15:36.313 --> 00:15:38.787
No, drops most slow or error traces.

230
00:15:39.067 --> 00:15:39.793
Cost:

231
00:15:40.073 --> 00:15:42.782
Cheapest, no buffering; decision propagates.

232
00:15:43.062 --> 00:15:44.065
Tail-based.

233
00:15:44.345 --> 00:15:45.411
When decided:

234
00:15:45.691 --> 00:15:47.163
After the trace completes.

235
00:15:47.443 --> 00:15:48.979
Keeps interesting traces?:

236
00:15:49.259 --> 00:15:52.182
Yes, keep all errors or slow, sample the rest.

237
00:15:52.462 --> 00:15:53.187
Cost:

238
00:15:53.467 --> 00:15:56.347
Buffers every trace until done (Collector memory).

239
00:15:57.697 --> 00:16:12.204
L3, 10,000 traces: head sampling at 1% kept only 6 of 554 slow traces; tail sampling kept all 554 plus a 1% baseline, at the cost of buffering.

240
00:16:12.484 --> 00:16:21.508
The common production setup: head-sample cheaply for baseline volume, and tail-sample in the Collector to guarantee errors and slow traces are kept.

241
00:16:21.788 --> 00:16:24.177
Whatever the rate, keep all error traces.

242
00:16:25.527 --> 00:16:26.935
Cardinality and cost.

243
00:16:27.215 --> 00:16:34.938
Cardinality (distinct label-value combinations) is the number-one cause of observability cost blowups and outages.

244
00:16:35.218 --> 00:16:39.314
Each unique combination of metric labels is a separate time series.

245
00:16:39.594 --> 00:16:48.618
L1: a counter labelled (service, level, userid) would create ~18,530 series; (service, level) only 10.

246
00:16:48.898 --> 00:16:58.135
Never put unbounded values in metric labels: user ids, order ids, trace ids, email, full URLs, raw error messages.

247
00:16:58.415 --> 00:17:02.021
They belong in logs and traces (searchable), not metrics.

248
00:17:03.371 --> 00:17:04.267
Symptoms:

249
00:17:04.547 --> 00:17:07.683
Prometheus OOM, slow queries, huge bills.

250
00:17:07.963 --> 00:17:14.875
Guardrails: limit label values, drop high-cardinality labels at scrape or ingest, and review new metrics.

251
00:17:15.155 --> 00:17:22.813
Logs and traces tolerate high cardinality (they're indexed differently), which is exactly why request ids go there.

252
00:17:24.163 --> 00:17:26.937
SLIs, SLOs, SLAs and error budgets.

253
00:17:27.217 --> 00:17:36.710
SLI (indicator): a measured ratio of good events, for example, goodrequests or totalrequests, or requests under 200 ms or total.

254
00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:38.505
Define good precisely.

255
00:17:38.785 --> 00:17:48.043
SLO (objective): a target for the SLI over a window, for example, 99.9% of checkouts succeed over 30 days.

256
00:17:48.323 --> 00:17:52.953
SLA (agreement): a contractual SLO with consequences (credits).

257
00:17:53.233 --> 00:17:55.665
Usually looser than the internal SLO.

258
00:17:57.015 --> 00:17:58.017
Error budget:

259
00:17:58.297 --> 00:17:59.663
1 minus SLO.

260
00:17:59.943 --> 00:18:03.761
At 99.9%, 0.1% may fail.

261
00:18:04.041 --> 00:18:17.503
L4: over 30 days that's 9,661 failed requests allowed; the month used 13,517 (140%, budget blown by one bad incident).

262
00:18:17.783 --> 00:18:25.399
The budget is the shared language between product (ship features) and reliability (stop and stabilize when the budget's gone).

263
00:18:25.679 --> 00:18:32.079
Choose SLIs the user feels (checkout success, page latency), not internal proxies (CPU).

264
00:18:32.359 --> 00:18:40.465
Aim SLOs at good enough that users are happy, not 100% (which is infinitely expensive and leaves no room to ship).

265
00:18:41.815 --> 00:18:43.031
Burn-rate alerting.

266
00:18:43.311 --> 00:18:46.703
Burn rate = how fast you're consuming the error budget.

267
00:18:46.983 --> 00:18:56.775
A burn rate of 1 exhausts a 30-day budget in exactly 30 days; 14.4 exhausts it in ~2 days; 6 in ~5 days.

268
00:18:57.055 --> 00:18:59.615
Alert on burn rate, not on a raw threshold.

269
00:18:59.895 --> 00:19:05.847
Google SRE's multi-window, multi-burn-rate pages when a fast and a slow signal agree:

270
00:19:06.127 --> 00:19:07.215
Fast (page):

271
00:19:07.495 --> 00:19:18.354
14.4 times over 1 h and 14.4 times over 5 min (the short window confirms it's still happening, avoiding alerting on an already-resolved spike).

272
00:19:18.634 --> 00:19:19.722
Slow (page):

273
00:19:20.002 --> 00:19:23.373
6 times over 6 h and 6 times over 30 min.

274
00:19:23.653 --> 00:19:28.645
Ticket (not a page): lower rates over longer windows (1 times, 3 times ).

275
00:19:29.995 --> 00:19:39.403
L4 compares strategies on the same 30 days (3 injected incidents: a sharp 20-min outage, a 6-h slow burn, a 3-min blip):

