306
00:20:22.497 --> 00:20:23.329
2 min.

307
00:20:24.679 --> 00:20:39.356
Naive fixed threshold: its pages don't map to budget, 1% error is a crisis at 600 rpm and noise at 50 rpm, so it both over- and under-pages, and it raised a false page on the harmless blip.

308
00:20:39.636 --> 00:20:50.089
Single long window: it flaps, the condition dips and re-crosses during an incident, turning one incident into 17 pages (the classic alert-fatigue generator).

309
00:20:51.439 --> 00:21:08.549
Multi-window multi-burn: fewest pages, zero false pages, ~2-min detection, and every page corresponds to real budget burn (L4's per-incident view: the sharp outage burned 108% of the month's budget; the blip, 1%).

310
00:21:09.899 --> 00:21:11.285
Good alerting practice.

311
00:21:11.565 --> 00:21:19.715
Page only on symptoms that need a human now (SLO burn, hard-down), not on causes (high CPU) that may be harmless.

312
00:21:19.995 --> 00:21:22.149
Causes become dashboards and tickets.

313
00:21:22.429 --> 00:21:24.947
Every page is actionable and has a runbook.

314
00:21:25.227 --> 00:21:27.403
If a page has no action, delete it.

315
00:21:27.683 --> 00:21:34.573
Reduce noise: dedup, group, inhibit downstream alerts when an upstream one fires, and set maintenance windows.

316
00:21:34.853 --> 00:21:40.208
Alert on the absence of signal too (no data = broken exporter, not all good).

317
00:21:41.558 --> 00:21:47.233
Review alerts after every incident: what paged that shouldn't have, what should have paged and didn't.

318
00:21:48.583 --> 00:21:50.225
Dashboards and correlation.

319
00:21:50.505 --> 00:22:02.708
Standard layout: an RED row per service (rate, errors, latency percentiles), a USE row for resources, an SLO or error-budget panel, and links out to logs and traces.

320
00:22:02.988 --> 00:22:12.887
Exemplars turn a spike on the latency graph into a click-through to the exact slow trace (L2 D emits the trace id of a slow request; L3 opens it).

321
00:22:13.167 --> 00:22:20.911
Design for the incident, not the demo: the first dashboard an on-call opens should answer what's burning and where, then drill down.

322
00:22:21.191 --> 00:22:23.793
Avoid wall-of-graphs dashboards nobody reads.

