702
00:49:50.757 --> 00:49:55.429
NTP; rely on span parent or child, not wall-clock (Ch 12).

703
00:49:56.779 --> 00:49:59.360
Observability of the observability stack.

704
00:49:59.640 --> 00:50:11.779
Meta-metrics: scrape success (up), ingestion rate, active series count (cardinality trend), dropped spans or logs, Collector queue depth, alert volume and the page-to-real-incident ratio.

705
00:50:12.059 --> 00:50:13.829
SLOs on the pipeline itself:

706
00:50:14.109 --> 00:50:19.123
95% of traces for errors are retained, metrics freshness 30 s.

707
00:50:19.403 --> 00:50:26.080
Cost dashboards: series per team, log GB per service, trace storage, with owners, so cost has an owner.

708
00:50:27.430 --> 00:50:28.369
Security.

709
00:50:28.649 --> 00:50:30.718
Telemetry carries sensitive data.

710
00:50:30.998 --> 00:50:42.433
Redact PII or secrets from logs (Ch 23), don't put customer identifiers in metric labels, and scrub span attributes (no tokens, no full addresses).

711
00:50:42.713 --> 00:50:52.526
Access control on dashboards and log or trace search: production logs can contain personal data; scope by team and audit access (Ch 36).

712
00:50:52.806 --> 00:50:59.185
Integrity: protect the audit-relevant logs (append-only or WORM) separately from debug telemetry.

713
00:50:59.465 --> 00:51:09.043
The pipeline is an attack surface: an open or metrics or trace endpoint leaks internals; require auth or network policy on scrape and ingest.

714
00:51:10.393 --> 00:51:12.057
Performance and tuning knobs.

715
00:51:12.337 --> 00:51:13.681
Here is a quick table:

716
00:51:13.961 --> 00:51:15.177
Knob, and Guidance.

717
00:51:15.457 --> 00:51:16.503
Scrape interval:

718
00:51:16.783 --> 00:51:22.841
15 to 30 s typical; 1 s only for short-lived debugging (L2 used 1 s).

719
00:51:23.121 --> 00:51:24.444
Histogram buckets:

720
00:51:24.724 --> 00:51:30.356
Cluster around SLO thresholds; use native or exponential histograms where available.

721
00:51:30.636 --> 00:51:31.980
Metric cardinality:

722
00:51:32.260 --> 00:51:36.143
Hard limits per metric; relabel-drop high-cardinality labels.

723
00:51:36.423 --> 00:51:37.788
Log level and sampling:

724
00:51:38.068 --> 00:51:43.124
Errors always; sample INFO by trace (L1); dynamic level per service.

725
00:51:43.404 --> 00:51:44.492
Trace sampling:

726
00:51:44.772 --> 00:51:49.828
Head baseline (1 to 10%) + tail keep-all-errors; cap Collector memory.

727
00:51:50.108 --> 00:51:51.047
Retention:

728
00:51:51.327 --> 00:51:53.972
Metrics: months (downsampled); logs:

729
00:51:54.252 --> 00:51:56.748
7 to 30 days; traces: days.

730
00:51:57.028 --> 00:51:58.500
Cardinality of traces:

731
00:51:58.780 --> 00:52:01.980
Limit span attributes; don't attach large payloads.

732
00:52:03.330 --> 00:52:04.055
Cost.

733
00:52:04.335 --> 00:52:12.101
Logs are usually the biggest bill, then traces, then metrics, but metrics blow up fastest via cardinality (L1).

734
00:52:12.381 --> 00:52:18.077
Control each: sample logs by trace, tail-sample traces, and bound metric labels.

735
00:52:18.357 --> 00:52:25.567
Downsample and tier: raw metrics for weeks, downsampled for a year; hot log storage for days, archived after.

736
00:52:25.847 --> 00:52:37.858
Observability commonly runs 5 to 15% of infra spend; the cost of not having it is the §1.1 incident measured in lost conversion and long MTTR.

737
00:52:39.208 --> 00:52:40.147
Part 7.

738
00:52:40.427 --> 00:52:42.133
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

739
00:52:43.383 --> 00:52:44.023
1.

740
00:52:44.303 --> 00:52:46.394
Charting and alerting on averages.

741
00:52:47.744 --> 00:53:01.099
Here is the PROMQL code. before: the mean hides the tail (L2: avg 74 ms, p99 1,545 ms). after: percentiles from the histogram.

742
00:53:03.949 --> 00:53:04.653
2.

743
00:53:04.933 --> 00:53:07.557
Logs with no correlation id (L1).

744
00:53:07.837 --> 00:53:13.149
Generate a trace id at the edge, propagate it, and log it on every line (MDC).

745
00:53:13.429 --> 00:53:16.010
Without it, cross-service debugging is guesswork.

746
00:53:16.290 --> 00:53:17.037
3.

747
00:53:17.317 --> 00:53:25.359
Free-text logs. log.info(processed order + id + for + user) can't be filtered or aggregated by field.

748
00:53:25.639 --> 00:53:27.517
Log structured key or values.

749
00:53:27.797 --> 00:53:28.501
4.

750
00:53:28.781 --> 00:53:31.341
High-cardinality metric labels (L1:

751
00:53:31.621 --> 00:53:34.010
18,530 series).

752
00:53:35.360 --> 00:53:46.795
Here is the Java code. before: userid or orderid or traceid as a label, one series each. after: bounded labels only; identifiers go to logs or traces.

753
00:53:49.645 --> 00:53:50.413
5.

754
00:53:50.693 --> 00:53:54.661
Threshold alerts on raw rates (L4: false pages, flapping).

755
00:53:54.941 --> 00:53:58.162
Alert on multi-window burn rate against an SLO.

756
00:53:58.442 --> 00:53:59.189
6.

757
00:53:59.469 --> 00:54:01.559
Paging on causes, not symptoms.

758
00:54:01.839 --> 00:54:06.042
CPU 80% pages at 3am for a healthy batch job.

759
00:54:06.322 --> 00:54:10.994
Page on user-facing SLO burn; make CPU a dashboard and a ticket.

760
00:54:11.274 --> 00:54:12.021
7.

761
00:54:12.301 --> 00:54:17.207
Buckets that miss the SLO (L2: +25% at p99).

762
00:54:17.487 --> 00:54:22.053
Put histogram buckets around your target latency, or use native histograms.

763
00:54:22.333 --> 00:54:23.015
8.

764
00:54:23.295 --> 00:54:26.815
Head sampling only, then trying to debug the tail (L3:

765
00:54:27.095 --> 00:54:30.146
6 or 554 slow traces kept).

766
00:54:30.426 --> 00:54:33.029
Tail-sample and always keep error traces.

767
00:54:34.379 --> 00:54:35.125
9.

768
00:54:35.405 --> 00:54:38.008
Optimizing off the critical path (L3).

769
00:54:38.288 --> 00:54:42.960
Trace first; speeding up a service that isn't on the critical path is wasted work.

770
00:54:43.240 --> 00:54:43.944
10.

771
00:54:44.224 --> 00:54:46.464
Alerts with no runbook and no action.

772
00:54:46.744 --> 00:54:51.437
If a page doesn't tell the on-call what to do, it trains them to ignore pages.

773
00:54:51.717 --> 00:54:54.277
Every alert links a runbook or is deleted.

774
00:54:54.557 --> 00:54:55.389
11.

775
00:54:55.669 --> 00:54:57.205
Dashboards nobody reads.

776
00:54:57.485 --> 00:55:00.152
A 60-panel wall is not observability.

777
00:55:00.432 --> 00:55:03.803
Start each dashboard with what's burning and where, then drill down.

778
00:55:05.153 --> 00:55:05.942
12.

779
00:55:06.222 --> 00:55:08.803
Not propagating context through async code.

780
00:55:09.083 --> 00:55:16.635
Spans and trace ids vanish across thread pools and message queues unless you propagate context (Ch 19:

781
00:55:16.915 --> 00:55:21.886
ScopedValue, context-wrapping executors; OTel context in Kafka headers).

782
00:55:23.236 --> 00:55:24.111
Part 8.

783
00:55:24.391 --> 00:55:25.735
Interview Q and A.

784
00:55:26.985 --> 00:55:27.817
Beginner.

785
00:55:28.097 --> 00:55:29.121
Question 1.

786
00:55:29.401 --> 00:55:32.003
Metrics, logs and traces, what's each for?

787
00:55:32.283 --> 00:55:33.649
Thirty-second answer:

788
00:55:33.929 --> 00:55:43.379
Metrics are cheap numeric time series (rates, gauges, latency histograms) for is it broken and how much, and drive dashboards and alerts.

789
00:55:43.659 --> 00:55:47.286
Logs are detailed structured events for what exactly happened.

790
00:55:47.566 --> 00:55:52.345
Traces follow one request across services for where did the time or error go.

791
00:55:52.625 --> 00:55:56.401
You need all three, sharing a trace id so you can pivot between them.

792
00:55:57.751 --> 00:55:58.753
Question 2.

793
00:55:59.033 --> 00:56:01.209
Why percentiles instead of averages?

794
00:56:01.489 --> 00:56:02.855
Thirty-second answer:

795
00:56:03.135 --> 00:56:04.607
Averages hide the tail.

796
00:56:04.887 --> 00:56:20.908
If most requests are fast but 1% take seconds, the average looks fine while some users have a terrible experience, and those are often the ones who abandon. p99 or p99.9 show what the worst-served users actually get.

797
00:56:21.188 --> 00:56:28.420
L2: a 74 ms average alongside a 1,545 ms p99.

798
00:56:29.770 --> 00:56:30.815
Question 3.

799
00:56:31.095 --> 00:56:34.253
What is a correlation or trace id and why does it matter?

800
00:56:34.533 --> 00:56:35.898
Thirty-second answer:

801
00:56:36.178 --> 00:56:45.671
A unique id generated when a request enters the system and propagated to every service it touches, logged on every line and attached to every span.

802
00:56:45.951 --> 00:56:51.221
It lets you retrieve everything about one request across all services with a single query.

803
00:56:51.501 --> 00:56:54.231
Without it, you can't reconstruct a request's path.

804
00:56:55.581 --> 00:56:56.648
Question 4.

805
00:56:56.928 --> 00:56:58.400
What are RED and USE?

806
00:56:58.680 --> 00:57:00.045
Thirty-second answer:

807
00:57:00.325 --> 00:57:01.904
RED for services:

808
00:57:02.184 --> 00:57:05.427
Rate, Errors, Duration, the health of request handling.

809
00:57:05.707 --> 00:57:06.987
USE for resources:

810
00:57:07.267 --> 00:57:11.853
Utilization, Saturation, Errors, for CPU, memory, pools, disks.

811
00:57:12.133 --> 00:57:17.211
RED tells you the service is unhealthy; USE tells you which resource is the cause.

812
00:57:17.491 --> 00:57:24.189
Google's four golden signals are latency, traffic, errors, saturation (RED + saturation).

813
00:57:25.539 --> 00:57:26.542
Intermediate.

814
00:57:26.822 --> 00:57:27.931
Question 5.

815
00:57:28.211 --> 00:57:32.585
How does a Prometheus histogram work, and why are tail quantiles approximate?

816
00:57:32.865 --> 00:57:34.230
Thirty-second answer:

817
00:57:34.510 --> 00:57:47.630
A histogram counts observations into cumulative buckets (le), plus sum and count. histogramquantile(q,...) finds the bucket containing the q-th observation and interpolates linearly within it.

818
00:57:47.910 --> 00:57:53.862
Accuracy depends on bucket width: where buckets are wide (usually the tail), the estimate is coarse.

819
00:57:55.212 --> 00:57:56.321
Question 6.

820
00:57:56.601 --> 00:57:59.481
How do you keep logs useful and affordable at scale?

821
00:57:59.761 --> 00:58:01.127
Thirty-second answer:

822
00:58:01.407 --> 00:58:16.105
Structure them (JSON or logfmt) so they're queryable by field; put a trace id on every line; sample by trace (keep a request's lines together) not by line; keep all errors; redact PII; and set retention by class.

823
00:58:16.385 --> 00:58:22.231
L1: sampling 1% of INFO traces cut volume to 3% while keeping every error.

824
00:58:23.581 --> 00:58:24.669
Question 7.

825
00:58:24.949 --> 00:58:27.594
What is cardinality and why is it dangerous?

826
00:58:27.874 --> 00:58:29.239
Thirty-second answer:

827
00:58:29.519 --> 00:58:35.706
Cardinality is the number of distinct label-value combinations, and each is a separate time series.

828
00:58:35.986 --> 00:58:44.733
Unbounded labels (user id, order id, URL) create millions of series, which OOMs the metrics store and inflates cost.

829
00:58:45.013 --> 00:58:45.845
L1:

830
00:58:46.125 --> 00:58:53.207
(service, level, userid), then ~18,530 series versus 10 for (service, level).

831
00:58:53.487 --> 00:58:57.327
Keep identifiers in logs and traces, not metric labels.

832
00:58:58.677 --> 00:58:59.701
Question 8.

833
00:58:59.981 --> 00:59:03.117
What is an SLI, SLO, SLA and error budget?

834
00:59:03.397 --> 00:59:04.763
Thirty-second answer:

835
00:59:05.043 --> 00:59:09.907
SLI is the measured ratio of good events (for example, checkout success).

836
00:59:10.187 --> 00:59:14.325
SLO is the target (99.9% over 30 days).

837
00:59:14.605 --> 00:59:18.595
SLA is a contractual SLO with penalties, usually looser.

838
00:59:18.875 --> 00:59:22.331
The error budget is 1 minus SLO, the allowed failure.

839
00:59:22.611 --> 00:59:28.669
It's the shared currency: within budget you ship features; burning it fast, you stop and stabilize.

840
00:59:30.019 --> 00:59:31.086
Question 9.

841
00:59:31.366 --> 00:59:33.670
Head versus tail sampling for traces?

842
00:59:33.950 --> 00:59:35.315
Thirty-second answer:

843
00:59:35.595 --> 00:59:46.027
Head sampling decides at the start (on the trace id, a fixed probability), cheap, no buffering, and the decision propagates, but it drops most slow and error traces.

844
00:59:46.307 --> 00:59:57.379
Tail sampling decides after the trace completes, so it can keep all errors and slow traces plus a baseline, at the cost of buffering every trace in the Collector until it finishes.

845
00:59:58.729 --> 00:59:59.497
Senior.

846
00:59:59.777 --> 01:00:00.823
Question 10.

847
01:00:01.103 --> 01:00:07.161
Design multi-window multi-burn-rate alerting for a 99.9% checkout SLO.

848
01:00:07.441 --> 01:00:08.807
Thirty-second answer:

849
01:00:09.087 --> 01:00:12.969
Burn rate = (error ratio in a window) / error budget.

850
01:00:13.249 --> 01:00:15.788
Page when a fast and a slow signal agree:

851
01:00:17.138 --> 01:00:18.269
Question 11.

852
01:00:18.549 --> 01:00:22.197
How do you use tracing to find why p99 latency is high?

853
01:00:22.477 --> 01:00:23.842
Thirty-second answer:

854
01:00:24.122 --> 01:00:30.159
Take slow traces and compute the critical path, the chain of spans that determines end-to-end time.

855
01:00:30.439 --> 01:00:32.103
Optimize only what's on it.

856
01:00:32.383 --> 01:00:39.487
Then aggregate many slow traces to see which service or operation is on the critical path most often at the tail.

857
01:00:39.767 --> 01:00:44.119
That names the p99 driver, which per-service metrics can't reveal.

858
01:00:45.469 --> 01:00:46.557
Question 12.

859
01:00:46.837 --> 01:00:50.144
How do you choose SLIs and SLOs for a new service?

860
01:00:50.424 --> 01:00:51.789
Thirty-second answer:

861
01:00:52.069 --> 01:01:03.973
Pick SLIs the user actually experiences, success ratio and latency of key journeys (checkout success, product-page 300 ms), not internal proxies like CPU.

862
01:01:04.253 --> 01:01:10.675
Set SLOs from what keeps users happy and the business healthy, with headroom to ship (not 100%).

863
01:01:10.955 --> 01:01:15.883
Define good precisely (which status codes, which latency threshold, measured where).

864
01:01:17.233 --> 01:01:18.513
Question 13.

865
01:01:18.793 --> 01:01:23.123
How do you propagate trace context across services and async boundaries?

866
01:01:23.403 --> 01:01:24.769
Thirty-second answer:

867
01:01:25.049 --> 01:01:39.449
Use W3C Trace Context: the traceparent header carries the trace id, parent span id and sampled flag over HTTP or gRPC, and trace headers ride in message metadata (Kafka headers).

868
01:01:39.729 --> 01:01:54.427
Within a process, the OTel context flows on the current thread; across thread pools and reactive pipelines you must explicitly propagate it (context-wrapping executors, ScopedValue, Ch 19), or spans detach.

869
01:01:55.777 --> 01:01:56.972
Question 14.

870
01:01:57.252 --> 01:02:00.516
What makes an alert good, and how do you fight alert fatigue?

871
01:02:00.796 --> 01:02:02.161
Thirty-second answer:

872
01:02:02.441 --> 01:02:11.380
A good alert is a symptom that needs a human now, is tied to an SLO per user impact, has a runbook, and fires rarely and precisely.

873
01:02:11.660 --> 01:02:26.785
Fight fatigue by: alerting on burn rate not thresholds (L4), grouping and inhibiting related alerts, deleting alerts with no action, alerting on missing data, and reviewing every incident's alerts afterward (what over or under-paged).

874
01:02:28.135 --> 01:02:29.031
Architect.

875
01:02:29.311 --> 01:02:30.506
Question 15.

876
01:02:30.786 --> 01:02:34.455
Design the observability strategy for the grocery platform.

877
01:02:35.805 --> 01:02:37.000
Question 16.

878
01:02:37.280 --> 01:02:40.629
How do you control observability cost without going blind?

879
01:02:41.979 --> 01:02:43.323
Question 17.

880
01:02:43.603 --> 01:02:49.939
Walk through debugging the §1.1 incident (conversion down, dashboards green) with your stack.

881
01:02:51.289 --> 01:02:52.463
Question 18.

882
01:02:52.743 --> 01:02:57.628
How do you make a system observable for unknown-unknowns, not just pre-built dashboards?

883
01:02:57.908 --> 01:02:59.273
Thirty-second answer:

884
01:02:59.553 --> 01:03:13.911
High-cardinality, correlated, queryable telemetry: wide structured events (many attributes per log or span), a trace id linking all signals, and tools that let you slice by arbitrary dimensions after the fact.

885
01:03:14.191 --> 01:03:20.313
Monitoring answers pre-set questions; observability lets you ask new ones during a novel incident.

886
01:03:20.593 --> 01:03:28.913
The enabler is that identifiers and context live in logs or traces (high cardinality) while metrics stay bounded for cheap alerting.

887
01:03:30.263 --> 01:03:31.501
Question 19.

888
01:03:31.781 --> 01:03:36.026
What's your rollout plan to add observability to a legacy Java service?

889
01:03:37.376 --> 01:03:38.485
Question 20.

890
01:03:38.765 --> 01:03:43.224
How does observability relate to reliability and the on-call experience?

891
01:03:43.504 --> 01:03:44.869
Thirty-second answer:

892
01:03:45.149 --> 01:03:48.115
Observability is what makes reliability operable:

893
01:03:48.395 --> 01:03:59.531
SLOs define the target, error budgets govern risk, burn-rate alerts turn budget threats into (rare, actionable) pages, and correlated signals make MTTR short.

894
01:03:59.811 --> 01:04:14.552
A good stack means the on-call is paged only when a human must act, opens one dashboard to see what's burning, and pivots via a trace id to the cause, instead of drowning in noise (L4) or staring at averages (L2).

895
01:04:15.902 --> 01:04:16.798
Part 9.

896
01:04:17.078 --> 01:04:18.486
Summary and Practice.

897
01:04:19.736 --> 01:04:20.824
Key takeaways.

898
01:04:21.104 --> 01:04:29.829
Monitoring answers known questions; observability lets you ask new ones, via correlated logs, metrics and traces sharing a trace id.

899
01:04:30.109 --> 01:04:34.312
Percentiles, never averages for user-facing latency (L2:

900
01:04:34.592 --> 01:04:40.907
74 ms avg hid a 1,545 ms p99).

901
01:04:41.187 --> 01:04:47.117
Structured logs with a correlation id on every line; sample by trace, keep errors (L1:

902
01:04:47.397 --> 01:04:50.320
3% volume, 100% of errors).

903
01:04:50.600 --> 01:05:00.861
RED for services, USE for resources; histograms with buckets around your SLO threshold (L2: p99 +25% otherwise).

904
01:05:02.211 --> 01:05:09.593
Cardinality is the cost or outage risk, identifiers go in logs or traces, not metric labels (L1:

905
01:05:09.873 --> 01:05:13.179
18,530 versus 10 series).

906
01:05:13.459 --> 01:05:19.881
Traces find the critical path; fix what's on it, not what looks slow (L3: fx-rates, not catalog).

907
01:05:20.161 --> 01:05:28.865
Sample traces tail-first for the interesting ones (L3: head 1% kept 6 or 554 slow; tail kept all).

908
01:05:29.145 --> 01:05:36.057
SLIs or SLOs or error budgets are the reliability contract and the ship or stop signal (L4).

909
01:05:37.407 --> 01:05:40.948
Alert on multi-window burn rate, not thresholds (L4:

910
01:05:41.228 --> 01:05:44.407
4 pages or 0 false versus 17 false).

911
01:05:44.687 --> 01:05:49.231
Every page is actionable with a runbook; review alerts after every incident.

912
01:05:50.581 --> 01:05:51.903
Decision cheat sheet.

913
01:05:52.183 --> 01:05:53.527
Here is a quick table:

914
01:05:53.807 --> 01:05:54.981
Need, and Choice.

915
01:05:55.261 --> 01:05:56.989
Is it broken, how much, trends:

916
01:05:57.269 --> 01:06:00.682
Metrics (RED or USE), cheap, low cardinality.

917
01:06:00.962 --> 01:06:02.562
What happened to this request:

918
01:06:02.842 --> 01:06:04.677
Structured logs with trace id.

919
01:06:04.957 --> 01:06:07.623
Where did time or errors go across services:

920
01:06:07.903 --> 01:06:09.973
Traces, critical-path analysis.

921
01:06:10.253 --> 01:06:11.639
Latency reporting:

922
01:06:11.919 --> 01:06:15.098
Percentiles from histograms, SLO-aligned buckets.

923
01:06:15.378 --> 01:06:16.850
Request identifiers:

924
01:06:17.130 --> 01:06:19.861
Logs or traces, never metric labels.

925
01:06:20.141 --> 01:06:21.549
Trace volume control:

926
01:06:21.829 --> 01:06:24.069
Head baseline + tail keep-all-errors.

927
01:06:24.349 --> 01:06:25.735
Reliability target:

928
01:06:26.015 --> 01:06:28.191
SLO + error budget per journey.

929
01:06:28.471 --> 01:06:29.303
Paging:

930
01:06:29.583 --> 01:06:32.719
